    ["Cytochrome P450 drug-metabolising enzymes are located mainly in the hepatocyte's:","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum",["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Mitochondrial matrix","Lysosomes"],"P450 enzymes are membrane proteins of the smooth reticulum and appear in the microsomal fraction. The rough reticulum makes secretory proteins such as albumin, which makes it the tempting confusion."],
    ["An 84-year-old needs an oral benzodiazepine before dental treatment. Which agent's clearance is least changed by ageing?","Lorazepam",["Diazepam","Chlordiazepoxide","Flurazepam"],"Lorazepam is cleared by glucuronidation, a phase II pathway that is well preserved with age. Diazepam, chlordiazepoxide and flurazepam rely on oxidation and form long-acting metabolites that accumulate."],
    ["A 61-year-old taking simvastatin drinks a large glass of grapefruit juice every morning and develops muscle pain. The most likely mechanism is:","Gut-wall CYP3A4 inhibition raising statin absorption",["Enzyme induction producing a toxic statin metabolite","Displacement of the statin from plasma albumin","Reduced renal excretion of the unchanged statin"],"Furanocoumarins inactivate intestinal CYP3A4, so more simvastatin reaches the blood and myopathy can follow. It is inhibition, not induction, and the kidney plays little part."],
    ["A patient wakes within minutes of a single IV dose of a lipid-soluble induction agent but recovers slowly after several repeated doses. The best explanation is that:","Tissue stores fill, so redistribution slows",["The liver enzymes are saturated by the first dose","Tolerance develops at the GABA-A receptor","The drug forms a longer-acting active metabolite"],"After one dose, the drug leaves the brain for muscle and fat. Once those stores are near equilibrium, the brain level can fall only as fast as the liver eliminates the drug, so recovery lengthens."]
